      },
      "worked": "The complementary-angle and negative-angle identities give $\\tan(90^\\circ-\\theta)=\\cot\\theta$, $\\cot(90^\\circ-\\theta)=\\tan\\theta$, $\\tan(90^\\circ+\\theta)=-\\cot\\theta$ and $\\cot(90^\\circ+\\theta)=-\\tan\\theta$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\n\\frac{\\tan(90^\\circ-\\theta)+\\cot(90^\\circ-\\theta)}{\\tan(90^\\circ+\\theta)-\\cot(90^\\circ+\\theta)}&=\\frac{\\cot\\theta+\\tan\\theta}{\\tan\\theta-\\cot\\theta}\\\\\n\\frac{\\tan(90^\\circ-\\theta)+\\cot(90^\\circ-\\theta)}{\\tan(90^\\circ+\\theta)-\\cot(90^\\circ+\\theta)}&=\\frac{\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta}{\\sin^2\\theta-\\cos^2\\theta}\\\\\n\\frac{\\tan(90^\\circ-\\theta)+\\cot(90^\\circ-\\theta)}{\\tan(90^\\circ+\\theta)-\\cot(90^\\circ+\\theta)}&=\\frac{1}{\\sin^2\\theta-\\cos^2\\theta}\\\\\n\\frac{\\tan(90^\\circ-\\theta)+\\cot(90^\\circ-\\theta)}{\\tan(90^\\circ+\\theta)-\\cot(90^\\circ+\\theta)}&=-\\frac{1}{\\cos^2\\theta-\\sin^2\\theta}.\n\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe original expression requires $\\sin\\theta\\ne0$, $\\cos\\theta\\ne0$ and $\\sin^2\\theta\\ne\\cos^2\\theta$. Thus $\\theta\\ne45^\\circ k$ for $k\\in\\mathbb Z$.",
